  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1045 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThree tropical systems bearing down on the U.S. this evening and\ntwo not too far away, but right now it doesn't look like we will\nsee much of an impact from any of the systems. Currently Isaias is\nmoving inland through Alabama and will have no impact on our\nweather. Closer to home Tropical Storm Rachel will approach the\nnorthern Baja California coast Saturday. This system quickly \nweakens and is absorbed by an approaching upper level trough as it\nmoves toward San Diego. The upper level trough shears most of the\nenergy and moisture off to our north and west so that system will\nhave little influence on our weather. The main weather maker for \nus on Saturday will be the aforementioned upper level trough \nwhich will pull some moisture into the area from northern Mexico \nand that will be enough to give is some widely scattered showers \nSaturday afternoon and evening. By Sunday the third tropical \nsystem will make landfall on the west coast of Mexico. This system\n(Simon) will have a tough time making it far inland as the \ncoastal mountains of Mexico, tear Simon up pretty well. What \nmoisture is advected to the north and northeast looks to stay just\nto the east of our region. So right now it looks like we will sit\nbetween the remnants of two tropical systems, but neither system \nlooks to have a big influence on our weather. We will again see a\nchance for isolated showers on Sunday and Monday, but those are \ndriven more by the upper level trough than by any tropical \ninfluence. High temperatures on Sunday will run well above average\neast of the Rio Grande while west of the Rio Grande we will see \nhighs running a few degrees below average.\n\nBy Tuesday the upper level trough will have swept by as it heads\neast and that will sweep most of the moisture and rain chances\nout of the region. We will see cooler temperatures for the middle\nof next week with high temperatures running at or a little below\naverage. Just beyond this forecast package, both the extended GFS\nand ECMWF indicate that a decent cold front will move into the \nregion from the east late Friday night which may knock our \ntemperatures well below average for next weekend.",
